$\angle EDC$ is supplementary to $\angle ADE,$ so $\angle EDC = 180^\circ - x.$

We have two triangles: $\triangle AED$ and $\triangle BEC$ are isosceles. Since $\triangle AED$ is isosceles, we have $\angle AED = \angle DEC2x.$

Since $\angle AED$ is supplementary to $\angle AED,$ we have $\angle AED = 110^\circ - x.$ Since $\angle A ED = \angle DEC = 2x,$ $x = 30^\circ.$

Therefore, $\angle EBC$ = $\angle EBA$ - $\angle CBA.$ $\angle EBA = 110^\circ - \angle AEB = 110^\circ - 2x = 50^\circ$ and $\angle CBA = 2 \angle ABE = 40^\circ$ (since $\angle AEB = 100^\circ$).

It follows that $\answer{\angle EBC} = \angle EBA - \angle CBA = \boxed{10^\circ}.$ </think> <answer> 10^\circ </answer>

